  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 4,
    "lesson_title": "Rewriting Signals as Functions of $\\tau$",
    "lesson_core_move": "Rewrite each signal's formula and active time interval using $\\tau$ as the integration variable so it can be placed correctly in the continuous-time convolution integral.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Rewriting Signals as Functions of $\\tau$",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Rewriting Signals as Functions of $\\tau$",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In continuous-time convolution, $\\tau$ is the variable that scans across the input timeline, while $t$ stays fixed as the output-time parameter. That is why the signals inside the convolution integral are written as functions of $\\tau$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "(x*h)(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "To rewrite a signal in $\\tau$, replace the independent variable in both places where the signal uses time: the formula and the active interval. Keep the constants, coefficients, endpoint values, and inequality directions the same.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=2t,\\quad 0\\le t\\le 3 \\quad \\longrightarrow \\quad x(\\tau)=2\\tau,\\quad 0\\le \\tau\\le 3",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This rewrite only prepares the signal for the convolution integral. It does not find overlap intervals, choose integration bounds, or evaluate the convolution.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Rewriting Signals as Functions of $\\tau$",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Replacing $t$ with $\\tau$ in Signal Formulas",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Rewrite the signal formula $x(t)=3t+1$ as a function of $\\tau$ for convolution setup.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that the signal is still written with $t$, but convolution setup needs the input signal written on the $\\tau$ axis. Here we are only rewriting the formula, so there is no active interval to change yet.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start with the original formula.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=3t+1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Replace the independent variable $t$ with $\\tau$. The coefficient $3$, the constant $1$, and the addition stay unchanged.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(\\tau)=3\\tau+1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the convolution-ready version of the formula is $x(\\tau)=3\\tau+1$. This step only changes the independent variable inside the signal formula; it does not introduce $t-\\tau$ or choose any integration bounds.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Rewriting Signals as Functions of $\\tau$",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Rewriting Active Time Intervals with $\\tau$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Rewrite the formula and active interval for $x(t)=4-t$ active on $1\\le t\\le 5$ using $\\tau$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that the signal is described in two places using $t$: the formula and the active interval. For convolution setup, both parts need to be written using the integration variable $\\tau$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=4-t,\\quad 1\\le t\\le 5",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "First replace $t$ with $\\tau$ in the formula. Then replace the interval variable $t$ with $\\tau$. The endpoint numbers $1$ and $5$ stay the same, and the inequality directions stay the same.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(\\tau)=4-\\tau,\\quad 1\\le \\tau\\le 5",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the prepared signal is $x(\\tau)=4-\\tau$ active on $1\\le \\tau\\le 5$. This step only rewrites the signal in the integration variable; it does not find overlap intervals or choose convolution bounds.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Rewriting Signals as Functions of $\\tau$",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Rewriting Shifted Signal Formulas with $\\tau$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Rewrite $x(t)=e^{-2(t-1)}$ active for $t\\ge 1$ as a function of $\\tau$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the shifted group $(t-1)$ inside the formula. Since this is still the original signal $x(t)$, the rewrite changes the signal's independent variable from $t$ to $\\tau$ while preserving the shift structure.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=e^{-2(t-1)},\\quad t\\ge 1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Replace $t$ with $\\tau$ inside the grouped expression, so $(t-1)$ becomes $(\\tau-1)$. Do not change the shift constant or reverse the order inside the parentheses.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{-2(t-1)}\\quad \\longrightarrow \\quad e^{-2(\\tau-1)}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Then rewrite the active interval with the same variable change. The condition $t\\ge 1$ becomes $\\tau\\ge 1$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(\\tau)=e^{-2(\\tau-1)},\\quad \\tau\\ge 1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the prepared signal is $x(\\tau)=e^{-2(\\tau-1)}$ active for $\\tau\\ge 1$. This is a rewrite of the given signal only; it does not introduce the convolution-specific form $t-\\tau$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Rewriting Signals as Functions of $\\tau$",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Preparing Piecewise Signals in $\\tau$ for Convolution Setup",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Prepare the piecewise signal $x(t)=0$ for $t<0$, $x(t)=t$ for $0\\le t<2$, $x(t)=2$ for $2\\le t\\le 4$, and $x(t)=0$ for $t>4$ as a function of $\\tau$ for convolution setup.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that the signal is piecewise: each row has a formula and a condition. To prepare it for convolution setup, rewrite every row using $\\tau$ while keeping the same order, endpoint numbers, endpoint inclusions, and zero pieces.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\begin{cases}0, & t<0\\\\ t, & 0\\le t<2\\\\ 2, & 2\\le t\\le 4\\\\ 0, & t>4\\end{cases}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Rewrite the formulas first. The constant pieces $0$ and $2$ stay unchanged. The formula $t$ becomes $\\tau$ because the independent variable is now $\\tau$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Then rewrite each condition by replacing $t$ with $\\tau$. The condition $t<0$ becomes $\\tau<0$, $0\\le t<2$ becomes $0\\le \\tau<2$, $2\\le t\\le 4$ becomes $2\\le \\tau\\le 4$, and $t>4$ becomes $\\tau>4$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(\\tau)=\\begin{cases}0, & \\tau<0\\\\ \\tau, & 0\\le \\tau<2\\\\ 2, & 2\\le \\tau\\le 4\\\\ 0, & \\tau>4\\end{cases}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the prepared signal is the complete piecewise definition of $x(\\tau)$ above. This step only rewrites the signal description; it does not combine pieces, find overlap, choose integration bounds, or evaluate a convolution.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
